{"candidates":[{"claim":"The proven optimal 12-input network (39 comparators, block 8a7a7d8f89f48b3e) can be extended to a 44-comparator 13-input sorting network by appending a 5-comparator insertion sequence for channel 12, exploiting the fact that channels 0–11 are already sorted for all 2^12 binary inputs.","counterconditions":["If no 5-comparator insertion sequence on channel 12 correctly places the 13th bit for all 2^13 binary inputs when appended to the specific S(12)=39 network, the construction yields a network of size >44 or fails to sort.","If the optimal 12-input network has an internal comparator ordering that interacts non-trivially with the 13th channel during the insertion phase (i.e., the 13th bit is not simply appended but must cross existing comparators), then the insertion model is invalid and the approach is unsound.","If a lower bound argument shows S(13) >= 45, then no 44-comparator network exists and this construction is impossible regardless of insertion sequence design."],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"8a7a7d8f89f48b3e","quote":"S(n): 0 1 3 5 9 12 16 19 25 29 35 39"},{"block":"6050cf69f391fa33","quote":"a 13-input candidate is verified by running all 2^13 = 8192 binary vectors through it"},{"block":"1194dff78a2795cf","quote":"A sorting network is therefore a straight-line program"},{"block":"843819388191d0e9","quote":"A candidate network is a list of (i, j) pairs with 0 <= i < j < 13"}],"mechanism":"Take the known S(12)=39 optimal network. After it runs, channels 0-11 are in sorted order for every binary input. The 13th channel (index 12) carries a single bit that must be placed into one of 13 possible positions. A binary-insertion-style comparator sequence performs this: first compare channel 12 with channel 6 (the median), then with channel 3 or channel 9 depending on the first outcome, then with the appropriate quartile, and so on. Because the 12 channels are already sorted, each comparator on (k, 12) acts as a conditional placement. A straight-line sequence of 5 such comparators (rather than the 4 rounds of a branching binary search) suffices because the network must handle all 13 insertion positions without branching, following the straight-line constraint described in block 1194dff78a2795cf. The resulting 44-comparator network is then verified by the 0-1 principle (8192 binary vectors, block 6050cf69f391fa33).","optional_refs":["src-c684127f981a8a8c1d5e0739c804e68436eebc3d"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["src-c684127f981a8a8c1d5e0739c804e68436eebc3d"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.3}],"simulation_proposals":[]}